    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d488-a1">

                <pb facs="0835" n="765" sameAs="frus:frus1883/pg765"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure in No. 127.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Folger to Mr. Frelinghuysen.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Treasury Department</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1883-01-08"><hi rendition="#italic">January</hi> 8, 1883</date>.
                        (Received January 10.)</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to transmit herewith for
                    your information, and such action as you may deem necessary, a copy of a letter
                    dated the 22d ultimo, with, two inclosures from Mr. E. Hubbs, a special agent of
                    this Department, from which it appears that a tax of $9,260 has been collected
                    by the Spanish vice-consul at Key West, Fla., on 23, 150 head of cattle,
                    exported from that port since November, 1881, to various Cuban ports by our
                    merchants, and that this tax was levied in the shape of fees, at the rate of 40
                    cents per head, for affixing the vice-consul’s signature to the manifests of the
                    exporting vessels.</p>
                <p>Very respectfully,</p>
                <closer><signed>CHAS. J. FOLGER,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Secretary of the
                            Treasury.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
